1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,072
Book Two, Proposition Nine.

2
00:00:02,072 --> 00:00:14,431
If a segment be cut into equal and unequal segments, the squares on the unequal segments of the whole are double of the square on the half and of the square on the segment between the points of section.

3
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:19,570
Let the segment A B be cut into equal segments at C, and into unequal segments at D.

4
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:31,018
From C, draw C E at right angles to A B by One Eleven, make C E equal to A C or C B by One Three, and join E A and E B.

5
00:00:31,018 --> 00:00:41,120
Through D, draw D F parallel to E C, and through F, draw F G parallel to A B by One Thirty-One, and join A F.

6
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:50,523
Because A C equals C E and the angle at C is right, the angles C A E and C E A are each half a right angle by One Five and One Thirty-Two.

7
00:00:50,523 --> 00:00:57,033
For the same reason, C E B and E B C are each half a right angle, so the whole angle A E B is right.

8
00:00:57,033 --> 00:01:06,147
By One Twenty-Nine and One Six, the small right triangles E G F and F D B are also isosceles, giving E G equal to G F, and F D equal to D B.

9
00:01:06,147 --> 00:01:13,893
By the Pythagorean theorem, One Forty-Seven, in right triangle A C E, the square on E A is double of the square on A C.

10
00:01:13,893 --> 00:01:21,314
And in right triangle E G F, the square on E F is double of the square on G F—which equals C D by One Thirty-Four.

11
00:01:21,314 --> 00:01:26,457
Therefore, the squares on A E and E F are double of the squares on A C and C D.

12
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:37,654
Since angle A E F is right, the square on A F equals the squares on A E and E F; and since the angle at D is right, the squares on A D and D F also equal the square on A F.

13
00:01:37,654 --> 00:01:43,643
Because D F equals D B, the squares on A D and D B are double of the squares on A C and C D.

14
00:01:43,643 --> 00:01:45,140
Which was to be proved.

15
00:01:45,560 --> 00:02:06,369
Proposition Two Nine marks a structural turning point in Book Two: where the first eight propositions rely purely on dissecting rectangles and gnomons, Euclid now invokes the Pythagorean theorem, One Forty-Seven, to prove the algebraic identity that the square of a plus b plus the square of a minus b equals twice the sum of a squared and b squared.

16
00:02:06,369 --> 00:02:18,320
